        <div class="docs-content">
            <section id="overview">
                <h2>Overview</h2>
                <p>Query Gate is a .NET 10 Blazor Server application for governed database access across SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL and MariaDB. Users work in a query workspace without seeing database credentials. Permissions, approvals and audit records control and document every execution.</p>
                <p>Key capabilities: permission-gated SQL with ScriptDom AST parsing, multi-engine support, granular object-level scoping and role boundaries, dynamic PII data masking (HIPAA, GDPR, PCI-DSS, SOC 2), visual query execution plans, time-boxed access grants, audit-first execution, passkey / TOTP authentication, an access-request workflow, and multi-format exports (CSV, Excel XLSX/XLSB, JSON, Markdown, and SQL INSERT scripts).</p>
            </section>
            <section id="user-guide">
                <h2>User guide</h2>
                <p>Query Gate provides engineers and auditors with controlled database access while eliminating credential sprawl.</p>
                <div class="docs-guide-cards">
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="terminal" /> Query workspace</h3>
                        <p>Select your database, write SQL with Monaco syntax assistance and keyboard shortcuts (<kbd>F5</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl+Enter</kbd>), and execute queries. Results appear in a responsive, filterable data grid with row counters.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="shield" /> SQL Gate validation</h3>
                        <p>Queries are parsed and verified using Microsoft ScriptDom (and dialect parsers) before execution. Disallowed statements, cross-database queries, or forbidden system procedures are denied upfront.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="key" /> Access requests &amp; approval</h3>
                        <p>Need temporary or elevated access? Request access directly from the Access page. Administrators review, approve with expiration dates, or reject requests with full audit records.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="download" /> Auditing &amp; multi-format export</h3>
                        <p>Every executed statement is recorded with duration, outcome, and user identity. Users with <code>EXPORT</code> permission can download query results as CSV, Excel (.xlsx, .xlsb), JSON, Markdown, or SQL INSERT statements.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="db" /> Object scoping &amp; boundaries</h3>
                        <p>Configure granular table and schema boundaries at both database and team-role levels. Protect sensitive tables with exclusion rules and wildcards like <code>dbo.*</code> or <code>*.secrets</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="docs-card">
                        <h3><Icon Name="lock" /> Dynamic PII masking</h3>
                        <p>Columns matching PII patterns (credit cards, SSNs, patient names, medical IDs) are redacted automatically. Users with the high-risk <code>UNMASK</code> permission see plain data.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
            <section id="object-rules">
                <h2>Object-level scoping and boundaries</h2>
                <p>Query Gate allows administrators to restrict access not just per database, but down to specific schemas and tables:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Database-level rules:</b> Applied to all users querying a database. Excluded tables (e.g. <code>dbo.AuditLogs</code>, <code>*.passwords</code>) cannot be queried by anyone, even administrators.</li>
                    <li><b>Role-level rules:</b> Applied to members of specific roles (e.g. Developers, Support, QA). Whitelists allow querying only approved tables, while blacklists protect confidential tables.</li>
                    <li><b>Wildcard syntax:</b> Use <code>*</code> for all objects, <code>schema.*</code> for an entire schema (e.g. <code>sales.*</code>), or <code>*.column</code> / <code>*.table</code> for cross-schema matching.</li>
                    <li><b>Conflict prevention:</b> Query Gate automatically detects if an object is simultaneously included and excluded, preventing accidental misconfigurations.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>
            <section id="data-masking">
                <h2>Dynamic data masking and compliance</h2>
                <p>Query Gate includes built-in compliance presets aligned with major regulatory standards:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>PCI-DSS 3.4 &amp; 3.2:</b> Masks credit card PANs (preserving only last 4 digits) and completely redacts CVVs and PINs.</li>
                    <li><b>HIPAA Safe Harbor:</b> Redacts patient names (<code>J***n S***h</code>), medical record numbers (MRNs), admission dates, and geographic locations smaller than a state.</li>
                    <li><b>GDPR &amp; CCPA:</b> Redacts email addresses, IP addresses, government IDs, and telephone numbers.</li>
                    <li><b>SOC 2 Confidentiality:</b> Redacts employee salaries, compensation figures, and authentication secrets.</li>
                    <li><b>The UNMASK permission:</b> Marked as High Risk in the access matrix; required to view unredacted data. All unmasked queries are recorded in the audit trail.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>
            <section id="exports-and-plans">
                <h2>Exports and query explain plans</h2>
                <p>Query results can be exported into multiple formats depending on your workflow:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>CSV (.csv):</b> Standard comma-separated format with formula injection neutralization.</li>
                    <li><b>Excel (.xlsx &amp; .xlsb):</b> Native OpenXML and binary spreadsheet formats with styled header rows.</li>
                    <li><b>JSON (.json):</b> Formatted array of objects for API and developer integration.</li>
                    <li><b>Markdown:</b> Clean GitHub-flavored Markdown table copied directly to clipboard.</li>
                    <li><b>SQL INSERT script:</b> Generates runnable <code>INSERT INTO</code> scripts with optional idempotency (<code>IF NOT EXISTS</code>) checks based on selected primary/unique keys.</li>
                    <li><b>Explain Plan:</b> Click <b>Explain</b> in the query workspace to generate and visualize estimated subtree costs, operator hierarchies, and execution steps.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>
            <section id="iis">
                <h2>Deploy to IIS on Windows</h2>
                <p>Install IIS, the WebSocket Protocol feature and the .NET 10 Hosting Bundle. Publish the project:</p>
                <pre><code>dotnet publish DbAccessManager.csproj -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained false -o ./publish</code></pre>
                <p>Deploy the published files, including <code>web.config</code>, to an IIS site at the domain root. Configure a dedicated application pool with No Managed Code, bind a trusted HTTPS certificate, and give the pool identity access to a persistent data protection key directory. Set the application configuration and run the first migration with one instance, as described in the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate/blob/main/docs/DEPLOYMENT.md#iis-on-windows">IIS guide</a>.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="azure">
                <h2>Deploy to Azure App Service</h2>
                <p>Create a Windows App Service with a .NET 10 runtime and connectivity to your SQL Server. Enable HTTPS Only and WebSockets, and use one instance. Publish the application and deploy the contents of the publish directory.</p>
                <p>Set the SQL connection, encryption and JWT keys, bootstrap credentials and a persistent data protection key path in App Service configuration. Apply migrations on the first start, then remove bootstrap credentials. See the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate/blob/main/docs/DEPLOYMENT.md#azure-app-service-windows">Azure guide</a> for settings and upgrade steps.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="linux">
                <h2>Deploy to a Linux server</h2>
                <p>Install the .NET 10 ASP.NET Core runtime, publish the application, and run it under a dedicated service account with systemd. The example uses Kestrel HTTPS directly with a trusted PFX certificate and a persistent key directory.</p>
                <p>Configure SQL Server access, secrets and migrations before starting the service. See the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate/blob/main/docs/DEPLOYMENT.md#linux-server-with-systemd">Linux guide</a> for a systemd unit and environment settings.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="docker">
                <h2>Deploy with Docker</h2>
                <p>Run Query Gate with Docker Compose. The setup includes a bundled SQL Server 2022 container, automatic database migrations, auto-generated persistent encryption keys, and a web setup wizard for creating the initial root administrator.</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Clone the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate">GitHub repository</a>.</li>
                    <li>(Optional) Copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code> to customize optional settings like database password or port. All variables are optional.</li>
                    <li>Start the stack:</li>
                </ol>
                <pre><code>docker compose up -d
docker compose logs -f query-gate</code></pre>
                <ol start="4">
                    <li>Open <code>http://localhost:5176</code> in your browser. (For remote hosts or production, see the HTTPS requirement below).</li>
                    <li>On first visit, Query Gate launches the setup wizard to create your root administrator account directly in the browser.</li>
                    <li>Sign in and explore your new governed workspace.</li>
                </ol>
                <p><strong>HTTPS requirement on remote hosts:</strong> Browsers treat <code>localhost</code> as a secure context, so plain HTTP works locally. When deploying to a remote server or domain, HTTPS is mandatory. Modern browsers block Blazor Server interactive circuits and antiforgery tokens on unencrypted HTTP, which prevents database registration and query execution from functioning.</p>
                <p><strong>External SQL Server:</strong> To connect to an existing database instead of the bundled container, set <code>DB_CONNECTION_STRING</code> in <code>.env</code>. Inside containers, <code>localhost</code> resolves to the container itself; on Docker Desktop use <code>host.docker.internal</code>.</p>
                <p>For detailed setup, upgrades, backups, and security notes, see the <a href="https://github.com/arjunreddy-001/query-gate/blob/main/docs/DOCKER.md">Docker deployment guide</a>.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="reverse-proxies">
                <h2>Reverse proxies and Coolify</h2>
                <p>When deploying in production, it is common to place Query Gate behind a reverse proxy such as Caddy, Nginx, Traefik, Cloudflare Tunnel, or a self-hosting platform like Coolify.</p>
                <p>Query Gate listens on plain HTTP port 5176 inside the container. The reverse proxy handles public HTTPS traffic, manages Let's Encrypt certificates, and forwards requests internally to port 5176.</p>
                <h3>Deploying on Coolify</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li>In Coolify, create a new Project and choose <strong>Docker Compose</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Connect your Git repository or paste the repository Compose configuration.</li>
                    <li>Set your domain name (for example, <code>querygate.yourdomain.com</code>). Coolify will automatically provision SSL certificates and route traffic to port 5176.</li>
                    <li>Deploy the stack and visit your domain to complete the initial setup wizard.</li>
                </ol>
                <h3>Forwarded headers</h3>
                <p>Configure your reverse proxy to forward client headers (<code>X-Forwarded-For</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>) so that client IP addresses and protocol schemes are preserved accurately in the audit log.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="configuration">
                <h2>Configuration and operations</h2>
                <p>All secrets must be kept private and backed up securely. Data protection and encryption keys are stored on the persistent <code>data-protection-keys</code> volume.</p>
                <table class="docs-table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Description</th><th>Default / Example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>PORT</code></td><td>Host port mapped to Query Gate. (Optional)</td><td><code>5176</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>DB_PASSWORD</code></td><td>SA password for the bundled SQL Server 2022 container. (Optional)</td><td><code>QueryGateDefault!2026</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>DB_CONNECTION_STRING</code></td><td>SQL Server connection string overriding the bundled container. (Optional)</td><td><code>Server=mssql;Database=QueryGate;...</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Encryption__Key</code></td><td>Base-64 key used to encrypt stored database connection strings. Auto-generated and persisted in volume if unset. (Optional)</td><td><code>openssl rand -base64 32</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Jwt__Key</code></td><td>Base-64 key used to sign session tokens. Auto-generated and persisted in volume if unset. (Optional)</td><td><code>openssl rand -base64 64</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>BootstrapAdmin__Email</code></td><td>Optional email for headless administrator creation. If omitted, the web setup wizard is used.</td><td><code>admin@company.com</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>BootstrapAdmin__Password</code></td><td>Optional password for headless administrator creation.</td><td>Min 12 chars, mixed case + digit</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Database__ApplyMigrationsOnStartup</code></td><td>Automatically apply EF Core migrations on startup. (Optional)</td><td><code>true</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>DataProtection__KeyPath</code></td><td>Persistent volume path for key files.</td><td><code>/var/query-gate/keys</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Keys stored in the persistent volume remain consistent across container restarts. Apply migrations on one instance at a time after a database backup.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="first-steps">
                <h2>First steps</h2>
                <p>Once deployed, create your administrator account on the setup screen or via optional environment variables on first start.</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Complete the initial setup wizard to create your root administrator account.</li>
                    <li>Register a target database (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, or MariaDB) using a dedicated, least-privilege account.</li>
                    <li>Create users and grant permissions or review their access requests.</li>
                    <li>Open the query workspace to run permitted SQL and review activity in the audit log.</li>
                </ol>
            </section>
            <section id="troubleshooting">
                <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
                <dl class="docs-dl">
                    <dt>Migrations fail on first start</dt>
                    <dd>The SQL account must have <code>db_owner</code> or schema-create permissions on the application database during migration. The bundled SQL Server uses the <code>sa</code> account with full permissions automatically.</dd>

                    <dt>Certificate errors behind a reverse proxy</dt>
                    <dd>When running behind a reverse proxy (such as Caddy, Traefik, or Nginx), terminate TLS at the reverse proxy and route plain HTTP to port 5176. Ensure your reverse proxy forwards headers correctly (X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Proto).</dd>

                    <dt>Cannot register database or run queries in workspace (HTTP on remote host)</dt>
                    <dd>Query Gate uses Blazor Server interactive circuits, antiforgery tokens, and secure cookies that require a secure context (HTTPS) on remote hosts. When accessed over plain HTTP on a remote domain or IP, browser security policies block circuit execution. Deploy behind an HTTPS reverse proxy (such as Cloudflare Tunnel, Coolify, Traefik, or Nginx).</dd>

                    <dt>"localhost" cannot reach SQL Server in Docker</dt>
                    <dd>Inside a container, <code>localhost</code> resolves to the container itself. Use the container service name <code>mssql</code> on the Docker network, or <code>host.docker.internal</code> (Docker Desktop) to reach a SQL Server running on the host machine.</dd>

                    <dt>Data protection key errors after restart</dt>
                    <dd>The key directory must be on a persistent volume. If the volume is deleted, authentication cookies and encrypted values become invalid. Mount the key directory as a named volume in your Compose file.</dd>

                    <dt>Users see "Account inactive or expired"</dt>
                    <dd>An administrator must activate the account and optionally set an expiry date. Check the Users admin page and ensure the account is active.</dd>

                    <dt>Five failed login attempts: account locked</dt>
                    <dd>Accounts lock for 15 minutes after five consecutive failed password attempts. An administrator can unlock the account early from the Users admin page.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>
